    "created_at": "2015-09-02T17:13:33Z",
    "updated_at": "2015-09-02T17:13:33Z",
    "body": "First, I'm not familiar with the tooling you use to generate the HTML spec, but I presume it's adding an id to the &lt;dfn>s? If not, you'll need to add one to each &lt;dfn> for Shepherd to consider it (it needs to be a valid anchor).\n\nSecond, you don't need the data-dfn-for=\"origin\" on the origin &lt;dfn>. data-dfn-for is only to link related &lt;dfn>s, like an attribute to an element, a CSS value to a property, or and IDL construct to its parent (like a method to an interface). \n\nIt's not clear to me if you need the data-dfn-for on the \"effective script origin\" or not, adding it will allow other specs to refer to \"origin/effective script origin\" which would only be useful to disambiguate it from other \"effective script origin\" &lt;dfn>s.\n\nThird, you might want to add a data-dfn-type attribute to assign a type to the &lt;dfn>s. Without that (unless it can derive a type from context) Shepherd will simply classify the anchor as a \"dfn\". A more appropriate type might be \"concept\" in this case. It's not critical but will help disambiguate the anchor from others that might share the name (like an \"origin\" IDL attribute or argument). If you need more types, let me know (the available types are listed in issue #20).\n\nNote that when multiple &lt;dfn>s are present, you can place the data-dfn-type and data-dfn-for attributes on a common ancestor rather than each individual &lt;dfn> (same with data-noexport). You can always override at any level in the tree.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
